From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Thu, 11 Sep 2026 23:30:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 90/90] local: drm/msm/dp: reprogram the pixel clock RCG on
 hibernate restore

THE eDP PANEL BUG. Confirmed on hardware: after a cross-boot hibernate resume
the eDP pixel clock runs at EXACTLY DOUBLE its programmed rate, so the panel is
driven at 120 Hz instead of 60 and presents nothing at all.

Read straight out of silicon at disp_cc + 0x82a0
(disp_cc_mdss_dptx3_pixel0_clk_src, mnd_width 16, hid_width 5):

                    M       N          resulting rate
  healthy  CFG=00002301  2903 / 15000  ->  261,270,000 Hz
  resumed  CFG=00002301  2903 /  7500  ->  522,540,000 Hz   exactly 2.000x

N is halved; M, CFG, src_sel and src_div are identical. The two values are
semantically exact: 261,273,600 is the 2944x1840@60 pixel rate divided by two
for DP widebus (2 pixels per clock), and 522,547,200 is the same mode at one
pixel per clock. So the clock is programmed for NON-widebus while the INTF
timing is widebus-halved (INTF_HSYNC_CTL=05e80010, htotal 1512, identical on
both boots). The arithmetic closes against measurement:

  frame = htotal 1512 * vtotal 2880 / rate
  healthy  16.67 ms -> 60.0 Hz     measured 16.72 ms / 59.8 Hz
  resumed   8.33 ms -> 120.0 Hz    measured  8.34 ms / 119.9 Hz

(measured two ways: modetest -v reported 59.8 vs 119.9 Hz, and 903 vs 1802
dpu_enc_frame_done_cb tracepoint intervals agreed.)

MECHANISM. msm_dp_ctrl_on_stream() and msm_dp_ctrl_on_stream_phy_test_report()
both call clk_set_rate(ctrl->pixel_clk, ...) with the correct rate on every
modeset. clk_core_set_rate_nolock() bails first:

	rate = clk_core_req_round_rate_nolock(core, req_rate);
	/* bail early if nothing to do */
	if (rate == clk_core_get_rate_nolock(core))
		return 0;

clk_core_get_rate_nolock() returns core->rate -- the CACHE, which came back
inside the hibernation image and therefore already equals the request. The RCG
is never reprogrammed and keeps whatever the loading kernel left in it.

Same defect class as 0034, 0068, 0069, 0077, 0079/0080, 0081 and 0083: cached
software state matching by construction while the silicon was reset underneath.

WHY IT HID FOR SO LONG. An early sweep reported "78/78 display clocks match
healthy" and killed the clock theory. It was measuring the cache:
clk_core_get_rate_recalc() only re-reads hardware when CLK_GET_RATE_NOCACHE is
set, and the kernel says so outright -- "Simply returns the cached rate of the
clk, unless CLK_GET_RATE_NOCACHE flag is set". debugfs clk_rate is NOT a
hardware read. Forcing 90 Hz did not help either, and that was filed as a red
herring; in fact both of this panel's modes share one pixel clock
(3024x1920x90 = 3024x2880x60 = 522,547,200), so a mode switch never changes the
request and never forces a reprogram. This hypothesis predicts that.

EVERY REMAINING SYMPTOM FOLLOWS. The panel locks the LINK (symbol lock, CR/EQ
done, interlane align, zero symbol errors over 10 s -- all measured) and refuses
to present an out-of-range timing, hence black. The external monitor showed a
partial picture because it is a 240 Hz-capable display fed at 240. The sink's own
frame CRC changed on a static image because frames really were arriving, just
unpresentable. Every DPU and DP register is byte-identical because the registers
are right -- the clock feeding them was not. And an mdss_gdsc power cycle could
not help: the RCG lives in disp_cc, outside the MDSS power domain.

CONFIRMED BY REPAIR, not just by reading: writing the healthy M/N back and
triggering CMD_UPDATE by hand made the panel present a picture for the first
time in this hunt.

THE FIX. Give the DP controller a real .restore that makes the framework's
cached rate disagree with the hardware, so the next modeset's clk_set_rate()
actually reprograms the RCG. The stream clock is off and the link is down at
.restore time, so parking the RCG at another rate briefly cannot glitch
anything. The remaining five system-sleep callbacks are spelled out rather than
left to SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS() so .restore can differ from .resume; they keep
exactly the behaviour they had. Same shape as 0069.

The dev_info() is load-bearing: one resumed boot says whether the cache was
actually poisoned, and dev_warn() fires if it was not.

KNOWN REMAINING ISSUE, NOT ADDRESSED HERE. With the rate corrected the panel
presents, and what it presents is a strip of correct pixels at the top then
INTF_UNDERFLOW_COLOR blue -- a genuine DPU underrun, exactly what the external
monitor showed all along. That is a SECOND, independent fault which was simply
invisible on the eDP while nothing could be presented at all. mdss_mdp_clk_src
is NOT the cause: its RCG reads CFG=00000105, src_div=5 on both boots.
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_ctrl.c    | 66 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_ctrl.h    |  1 +
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_display.c | 25 +++++++++--
 3 files changed, 90 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff -ur a4/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_ctrl.c b4/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_ctrl.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_ctrl.c	2026-09-11 20:12:27.435914036 +0300
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_ctrl.c	2026-09-11 20:12:27.457558875 +0300
@@ -202,6 +202,70 @@
 /*
  * NOTE: resetting DP controller will also clear any pending HPD related interrupts
  */
+/*
+ * Force the next clk_set_rate() on the pixel clock to actually reprogram the
+ * RCG, by making the clk framework's cached rate disagree with it.
+ *
+ * After a cross-boot hibernate restore the cache comes back inside the
+ * hibernation image, so it already equals whatever the next modeset will ask
+ * for, and clk_core_set_rate_nolock() short-circuits:
+ *
+ *	rate = clk_core_req_round_rate_nolock(core, req_rate);
+ *	// bail early if nothing to do
+ *	if (rate == clk_core_get_rate_nolock(core))
+ *		return 0;
+ *
+ * clk_core_get_rate_nolock() returns core->rate, the cache -- not the hardware.
+ * So the RCG keeps whatever the loading kernel left in it.
+ *
+ * Measured on x1e80100 (Yoga Slim 7x, eDP on dptx3), reading disp_cc + 0x82a0
+ * straight out of silicon:
+ *
+ *	healthy   M=2903 N=15000 -> 261,270,000 Hz   widebus, 2 px/clock
+ *	resumed   M=2903 N= 7500 -> 522,540,000 Hz   1 px/clock, exactly 2.000x
+ *
+ * The INTF timing is widebus-halved on both boots (INTF_HSYNC_CTL=05e80010,
+ * htotal 1512), so htotal * vtotal / rate gives 16.67 ms against 8.33 ms --
+ * matching a measured 59.8 Hz against 119.9 Hz. 120 Hz is out of range for this
+ * 90 Hz panel: the sink locks the LINK (symbol lock, CR/EQ done, zero symbol
+ * errors, all measured) and simply never presents the video. Writing the healthy
+ * M/N back by hand and triggering CMD_UPDATE made the panel light up.
+ *
+ * Called from .restore, where the stream clock is off and the link is down, so
+ * briefly parking the RCG at another rate cannot glitch anything.
+ */
+void msm_dp_ctrl_pixel_clk_invalidate(struct msm_dp_ctrl *msm_dp_ctrl)
+{
+	struct msm_dp_ctrl_private *ctrl;
+	unsigned long cached, parked;
+
+	ctrl = container_of(msm_dp_ctrl, struct msm_dp_ctrl_private, msm_dp_ctrl);
+
+	if (!ctrl->pixel_clk)
+		return;
+
+	cached = clk_get_rate(ctrl->pixel_clk);
+	if (!cached)
+		return;
+
+	/*
+	 * A DP pixel RCG computes M/N for arbitrary rates, so half the cached
+	 * value is always representable and always differs. Verify it, because
+	 * a silent no-op here would leave the original bug in place.
+	 */
+	clk_set_rate(ctrl->pixel_clk, cached / 2);
+	parked = clk_get_rate(ctrl->pixel_clk);
+
+	if (parked == cached)
+		dev_warn(ctrl->dev,
+			 "hibernate restore: pixel clk cache stuck at %lu Hz; the 2x RCG bug may persist\n",
+			 cached);
+	else
+		dev_info(ctrl->dev,
+			 "hibernate restore: pixel clk cache %lu -> %lu Hz, next modeset will reprogram the RCG\n",
+			 cached, parked);
+}
+
 void msm_dp_ctrl_reset(struct msm_dp_ctrl *msm_dp_ctrl)
 {
 	struct msm_dp_ctrl_private *ctrl =
diff -ur a4/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_ctrl.h b4/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_ctrl.h
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_ctrl.h	2026-09-11 20:12:27.439871010 +0300
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_ctrl.h	2026-09-11 20:12:27.457633094 +0300
@@ -32,6 +32,7 @@
 				    void __iomem *link_base);
 
 void msm_dp_ctrl_reset(struct msm_dp_ctrl *msm_dp_ctrl);
+void msm_dp_ctrl_pixel_clk_invalidate(struct msm_dp_ctrl *msm_dp_ctrl);
 void msm_dp_ctrl_phy_init(struct msm_dp_ctrl *msm_dp_ctrl);
 void msm_dp_ctrl_phy_exit(struct msm_dp_ctrl *msm_dp_ctrl);
 void msm_dp_ctrl_irq_phy_exit(struct msm_dp_ctrl *msm_dp_ctrl);
diff -ur a4/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_display.c b4/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_display.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_display.c	2026-09-11 20:12:27.443573918 +0300
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_display.c	2026-09-11 20:12:27.457702731 +0300
@@ -1244,10 +1244,42 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_PM_SLEEP
+/*
+ * Hibernate restore. The clk framework's cached rates came back in the image
+ * and already match what the next modeset will request, so clk_set_rate() on
+ * the pixel clock short-circuits and the RCG keeps whatever the loading kernel
+ * left in it -- measured at exactly 2x on x1e80100, which drives the panel at
+ * 120 Hz and makes it present nothing at all. See
+ * msm_dp_ctrl_pixel_clk_invalidate().
+ *
+ * Doing this in .restore rather than .resume keeps it off the s2idle path,
+ * where one kernel owns the hardware throughout and the cache is honest.
+ */
+static int msm_dp_pm_restore(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct msm_dp_display_private *dp = dev_get_dp_display_private(dev);
+
+	msm_dp_ctrl_pixel_clk_invalidate(dp->ctrl);
+
+	return pm_runtime_force_resume(dev);
+}
+#endif
+
+/*
+ * Spelled out rather than using SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS() so .restore can
+ * differ from .resume; the other five keep exactly the behaviour they had.
+ */
 static const struct dev_pm_ops msm_dp_pm_ops = {
 	SET_RUNTIME_PM_OPS(msm_dp_pm_runtime_suspend, msm_dp_pm_runtime_resume, NULL)
-	SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(pm_runtime_force_suspend,
-				pm_runtime_force_resume)
+#ifdef CONFIG_PM_SLEEP
+	.suspend  = pm_runtime_force_suspend,
+	.resume   = pm_runtime_force_resume,
+	.freeze   = pm_runtime_force_suspend,
+	.thaw     = pm_runtime_force_resume,
+	.poweroff = pm_runtime_force_suspend,
+	.restore  = msm_dp_pm_restore,
+#endif
 };
 
 static struct platform_driver msm_dp_display_driver = {
-- 
